Anaphylactic Shock (Jack Jumper Ant Sting)
Hydration, Fluid and Volume abnormalities Distributive Shock
Sharp localized sting pain followed by wheal, Sudden cardiovascular collapse/syncope, Generalized urticaria/bronchospasm

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HFV-006
Reason for Visit: Sudden loss of consciousness and rapid development of systemic allergic symptoms following an insect sting, necessitating emergency intervention to arrest evolving distributive shock.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a witnessed collapse immediately after a painful insect sting, coupled with difficulty breathing and full-body rash. This indicated an imminent life-threatening emergency requiring an immediately urgent, on-site medical evaluation and administration of intramuscular adrenaline to prevent fatal cardiovascular collapse prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Sudden collapse and breathing difficulty after an ant sting. Patient states, My leg felt like it was on fire, then everything went black and now my chest is closing over.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms began precisely 20 minutes ago while the patient was gardening. There was immediate sharp burning pain at the sting site on the right ankle, quickly followed by the rapid development of a large local erythematous wheal. Within five minutes, the patient experienced a sudden precipitous drop in blood pressure causing a brief syncopal episode witnessed by the spouse. Upon regaining consciousness, a generalized urticarial rash and profound diaphoresis had developed. The patient now complains of severe bronchoconstriction presenting as acute shortness of breath and an audible wheeze, alongside nausea and severe abdominal cramping.
Functional Status: Supine on the floor, unable to sit up due to severe orthostatic instability and respiratory distress.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate life-threat due to systemic anaphylaxis; spouse is highly distressed but following instructions.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 85/55 mmHg | HR: 125 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 28 bpm | SpO2: 92% | Wt: 75.0 Kg
General: Patient is pale, diaphoretic, agitated, and in visible respiratory distress.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, weak peripheral pulses, and overt hypotension indicative of profound vasodilation. Capillary refill is 4 seconds. Respiratory: Bilateral widespread expiratory wheeze with accessory muscle use. Integumentary: The diagnostic triad is evident: a sharp localized sting pain followed by wheal (evident on the right ankle), history of sudden cardiovascular collapse/syncope, and objective generalized urticaria/bronchospasm. Generalized urticaria covers the trunk and limbs. Angioedema is noted involving the lips and periorbital tissues.
Environmental Observations: Patient is lying on the lawn where the event occurred; area cleared for safe treatment and incoming paramedic access.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No previous history of venom allergy documented.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Anaphylactic Shock (Jack Jumper Ant Sting) - Status: Acute/Life-threatening
Differentials:

Vasovagal Syncope - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of severe generalized urticaria, marked angioedema, and prominent bronchospasm, which are entirely absent in simple vasovagal events.

Localized Allergic Reaction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Systemic involvement including respiratory compromise (wheeze) and cardiovascular collapse (hypotension, syncope).

Acute Asthma Exacerbation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Sudden precipitous drop in blood pressure, cutaneous manifestations, and clear trigger (sting), which define anaphylaxis rather than isolated asthma.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits an acute, massive type 1 IgE-mediated hypersensitivity reaction triggered by a Jack Jumper ant sting. The precipitous cardiovascular collapse, generalized urticaria, and acute bronchoconstriction fulfill all criteria for severe anaphylactic shock with profound distributive fluid shifts. Delaying intervention until the next in-hours period would have resulted in fatal hypoxia or cardiac arrest. Urgent, immediate after-hours assessment and the prompt administration of intramuscular adrenaline were critical to reverse systemic vasodilation and severe airway edema, serving as a life-saving bridge until emergency medical services arrived. The expected investigation results include the presence of Jack Jumper ant-specific IgE antibodies on serological radioallergosorbent testing that would assist in confirming the diagnosis at a later outpatient immunology review.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Adrenaline 500mcg (0.5mg of 1:1000) Intramuscularly into the anterolateral thigh. Maintained patient in a supine position with legs elevated. Administered high-flow oxygen via non-rebreather mask. Handover completed to arriving ambulance crew.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent future life-threatening emergencies, you must commit to physically carrying your prescribed adrenaline auto-injectors whenever you leave the house. Place them in the same spot as your keys and phone; when you grab your keys, pick up the injectors and verbally state, "I have my EpiPen." The immediate reduction in anxiety regarding outdoor activities will serve as the reward for building this life-saving habit.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Adrenaline auto-injectors required post-discharge.
Follow-up: Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department for observation and management of potential biphasic reactions. Mandatory referral to an Immunologist for venom immunotherapy evaluation.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Patient is currently under the care of emergency services. Post-discharge, if any symptoms of lip swelling, difficulty breathing, or widespread rash return, use the adrenaline auto-injector immediately and call 000.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Anaphylaxis is a dynamic, life-threatening condition requiring prolonged clinical observation in a hospital setting.
Clinical Course: Adrenaline halts the immediate, severe reaction. However, a secondary wave of symptoms (biphasic reaction) can occur up to 72 hours later, making hospital observation mandatory.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not stand up or sit up under any circumstances during an allergic reaction, as this can cause sudden cardiac arrest. Always lie flat with your legs elevated.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The adrenaline injection acts immediately to squeeze your blood vessels, raising your blood pressure, and opens your lung passages so you can breathe.
Actionable Steps: Remain flat. Upon discharge from the hospital, practice with your auto-injector trainer daily until the action becomes automatic muscle memory.

